To safely access nested properties in a JavaScript object, you can use optional chaining, which allows you to read the value of deeply nested properties without having to explicitly check each level for the existence of the property. If any part of the chain is undefined or null, it will short-circuit and return undefined, preventing runtime errors.

Here's an example:

```javascript
// Let's consider a user object with nested properties
const user = {
    name: 'Alice',
    address: {
        street: '123 Main St',
        city: 'Wonderland'
    }
};

// Traditional way with manual checks
let streetName = user && user.address && user.address.street;
console.log(streetName); // Outputs: '123 Main St'

// Using optional chaining
streetName = user?.address?.street;
console.log(streetName); /

